WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.083
(fire alarm ringing)

00:00:04.185 --> 00:00:05.718
- [Ramona] Every day,
whenever I hear that buzzer,

00:00:05.718 --> 00:00:07.191
it just gets your blood flowing,

00:00:07.191 --> 00:00:08.290
your heart pumping,

00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:10.163
and you just take off at a sprint.

00:00:10.163 --> 00:00:10.996
(propellers whirring)

00:00:10.996 --> 00:00:12.813
You really don't know
what your getting into

00:00:12.813 --> 00:00:15.201
until you can see the fire.

00:00:15.201 --> 00:00:16.422
(man mumbling on headset)

00:00:16.422 --> 00:00:17.441
You're jumping out of a plane.

00:00:17.441 --> 00:00:19.967
That's always gonna induce
a little bit of adrenaline.

00:00:19.967 --> 00:00:22.717
(dramatic music)

00:00:29.576 --> 00:00:30.592
- [Narrator] There are few professions

00:00:30.592 --> 00:00:34.252
that captivate us quite like smokejumping,

00:00:34.252 --> 00:00:36.203
and I'm speaking with a
woman who has been doing it

00:00:36.203 --> 00:00:37.548
for over a decade.

00:00:37.548 --> 00:00:39.321
- [Ramona] My name is Ramona Beyuka,

00:00:39.321 --> 00:00:42.857
and I am a McCall
smokejumper in McCall, Idaho.

00:00:42.857 --> 00:00:44.048
- [Narrator] She jumps out of planes

00:00:44.048 --> 00:00:45.595
and into forest fires.

00:00:45.595 --> 00:00:47.955
Her job is to contain them.

00:00:47.955 --> 00:00:51.072
This is what a day for
a smokejumper is like.

00:00:51.072 --> 00:00:53.004
(dramatic music)

00:00:53.004 --> 00:00:55.534
- [Ramona] You fly in a Twin
Otter, with the door off.

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:56.866
We're all wearing ear plugs,

00:00:56.866 --> 00:00:59.627
and everyone's kind of in
their own world at that point.

00:00:59.627 --> 00:01:02.536
- [Narrator] And this flight
can take up to a few hours.

00:01:02.536 --> 00:01:05.346
But it becomes clear when they're close.

00:01:05.346 --> 00:01:07.292
- [Ramona] Usually, you smell that smoke.

00:01:07.292 --> 00:01:09.868
If it's that tiny, wood-burning smell,

00:01:09.868 --> 00:01:12.118
you know that it's go time.

00:01:13.605 --> 00:01:14.704
You get in the door,

00:01:14.704 --> 00:01:16.882
and that's when you really gotta focus.

00:01:16.882 --> 00:01:17.715
(woman exhales slowly)

00:01:17.715 --> 00:01:18.871
Your heart is pounding,

00:01:18.871 --> 00:01:21.440
and then they slap you on your shoulder.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.903
I don't even really think about it.

00:01:22.903 --> 00:01:25.045
I just go out of the plane.

00:01:25.045 --> 00:01:29.808
(electronic alarm blaring)
(heart pounding)

00:01:29.808 --> 00:01:33.685
I try not look at the ground,
or the fire, at that point.

00:01:33.685 --> 00:01:35.762
You're just super-focused.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:40.203
(electronic alarm blaring)
(heart pounding)

00:01:40.203 --> 00:01:42.685
- [Narrator] And that was just part one.

00:01:42.685 --> 00:01:44.403
Now they fight the fire.

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:46.261
(airplane propellers whirring)
(fire crackling)

00:01:46.261 --> 00:01:48.070
- [Ramona] I've been to
so many forest fires,

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:50.050
and yet every jump is different.

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:52.825
- [Narrator] But it's not
a profession for everyone.

00:01:52.825 --> 00:01:53.695
- [Ramona] That adrenaline is something

00:01:53.695 --> 00:01:56.803
that's a big part of
keeping me coming back.

00:01:56.803 --> 00:01:58.083
I do stop and think,

00:01:58.083 --> 00:02:00.594
"Wow, I am really lucky
to be in this job."

00:02:00.594 --> 00:02:03.344
(dramatic music)

